## Asking about an address

```cpp
MEMORY_BASIC_INFORMATION info{};
VirtualQuery(someAddress, &info, sizeof(info));
```

`VirtualQuery` fills a `MEMORY_BASIC_INFORMATION` describing the **region** containing the address: a run of consecutive pages that all share the same state, protection and type.

| Field | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `BaseAddress` | Start of the region containing the address |
| `RegionSize` | Size of the region, in bytes |
| `AllocationBase` | Start of the original `VirtualAlloc` (or mapping) the region belongs to |
| `State` | `MEM_FREE`, `MEM_RESERVE` or `MEM_COMMIT` (lesson 6.03) |
| `Protect` | Current protection, such as `PAGE_READWRITE` |
| `Type` | `MEM_IMAGE` (an exe or DLL), `MEM_MAPPED` (a file mapping) or `MEM_PRIVATE` (heap, stack, `VirtualAlloc`) |

## What is this pointer?

The `Type` and `AllocationBase` fields identify where any pointer leads. For an image, `AllocationBase` is the module's `HMODULE`, so `GetModuleFileNameW` gives its name:

```cpp
#include <windows.h>
#include <iostream>
#include <memory>
#include <string>

const char* kind(const void* p)
{
    MEMORY_BASIC_INFORMATION info{};
    if (!VirtualQuery(p, &info, sizeof(info)) || info.State != MEM_COMMIT) return "not committed memory";
    if (info.Type == MEM_IMAGE)
        return (info.Protect & (PAGE_EXECUTE | PAGE_EXECUTE_READ | PAGE_EXECUTE_READWRITE)) ? "code in a module" : "data in a module";
    if (info.Type == MEM_MAPPED) return "a mapped file";
    return "private memory (heap, stack or VirtualAlloc)";
}

int g_global{ 1 };

int main()
{
    int local{ 2 };
    auto heap{ std::make_unique<int>(3) };
    std::cout << "main:       " << kind(reinterpret_cast<const void*>(&main)) << '\n';
    std::cout << "g_global:   " << kind(&g_global) << '\n';
    std::cout << "a literal:  " << kind("text") << '\n';
    std::cout << "local:      " << kind(&local) << '\n';
    std::cout << "heap:       " << kind(heap.get()) << '\n';
    std::cout << "nullptr+16: " << kind(reinterpret_cast<const void*>(16)) << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```
```
main:       code in a module
g_global:   data in a module
a literal:  data in a module
local:      private memory (heap, stack or VirtualAlloc)
heap:       private memory (heap, stack or VirtualAlloc)
nullptr+16: not committed memory
```

A crash reporter or a debugging tool does exactly this to say "the bad pointer pointed into freed space" or "the crash was in plugin.dll's code".

## Walking the whole address space

Regions are consecutive, so you can walk them all: start at address 0, query, then jump to `BaseAddress + RegionSize`, until `VirtualQuery` fails at the end of user space.

```cpp
#include <windows.h>
#include <iostream>

int main()
{
    SIZE_T committed{ 0 }, reserved{ 0 };
    int regions{ 0 }, imageRegions{ 0 };
    MEMORY_BASIC_INFORMATION info{};
    for (const char* at{ nullptr }; VirtualQuery(at, &info, sizeof(info)) == sizeof(info); at = static_cast<const char*>(info.BaseAddress) + info.RegionSize)
    {
        ++regions;
        if (info.State == MEM_COMMIT) committed += info.RegionSize;
        if (info.State == MEM_RESERVE) reserved += info.RegionSize;
        if (info.State == MEM_COMMIT && info.Type == MEM_IMAGE) ++imageRegions;
    }
    std::cout << regions << " regions, " << imageRegions << " committed image regions\n";
    std::cout << committed / 1024 << " KB committed, " << reserved / (1024 * 1024) << " MB reserved\n";
    return 0;
}
```
```varies
212 regions, 63 committed image regions
6044 KB committed, 2056 MB reserved
```

Even a tiny program has a couple of hundred regions: every DLL contributes several (one per section with a different protection: code, read-only data, writable data), plus heaps, the stack and its guard page, and areas Windows reserves for itself. The reserved total is large because of reservations such as the stack's 1 MB and heap segments, which cost no memory.

## Reading the map

A typical slice of the map, written out the way the practice task does:

```
00000097A1A00000  1004 KB  reserved   private              thread stack (reserved part)
00000097A1AFB000    12 KB  rw- guard  private              stack guard page
00000097A1AFE000     8 KB  rw-        private              stack (committed part)
0000022F7B4A0000  1024 KB  rw-        private              heap segment
00007FF6A1200000     4 KB  r--        image  game.exe      headers
00007FF6A1201000   108 KB  r-x        image  game.exe      .text
00007FF6A121C000    40 KB  r--        image  game.exe      .rdata
00007FF6A1226000     8 KB  rw-        image  game.exe      .data
```

The sections of `game.exe` from lesson 2.03 are visible directly as regions with their protections. The thread stack shows reserve-then-commit-as-it-grows with its guard page from lesson 6.03.

Microsoft's **VMMap** (part of the free Sysinternals tools) shows this map for any process with a friendly interface, and **Visual Studio's** diagnostic tools can too. Writing the walk yourself is the best way to understand what they show.

## Common mistakes

> [!warning] Not advancing past free regions
> Free regions are regions too. Always step by `BaseAddress + RegionSize`, whatever the state.

> [!warning] Treating `Protect` as meaningful for free or reserved regions
> It only describes committed pages; for reserved regions, `AllocationProtect` holds the protection the reservation was made with.

> [!warning] Reading memory just because `VirtualQuery` said it was committed
> Another thread can free it a moment later. Tools that read arbitrary memory must be prepared for the read to fail (chapter 12).

## Check yourself
```quiz
? What three memory types does `VirtualQuery` report, and what is each?
+ `MEM_IMAGE` (a loaded exe or DLL), `MEM_MAPPED` (a mapped file) and `MEM_PRIVATE` (heaps, stacks, `VirtualAlloc`)
- `MEM_STACK`, `MEM_HEAP` and `MEM_CODE`, by what the memory is used for
- `MEM_FREE`, `MEM_RESERVE` and `MEM_COMMIT`, by whether the memory exists
- `MEM_READ`, `MEM_WRITE` and `MEM_EXECUTE`, by protection
> Free, reserved and committed are the page states, reported separately; the type says what backs the memory.

? Why does one DLL show up as several regions?
+ Its sections have different protections, and regions share one
- Windows loads a DLL in several pieces to save memory
- Each function in the DLL gets its own region
- One region per thread that uses the DLL
> Code is execute-read, constants read-only and data read-write, so the DLL splits into a region for each.

? How can a tool tell which module a code address belongs to?
- Look the address up in the program's `.pdb`
- Divide the address by the page size to get a module number
+ Query it; a `MEM_IMAGE` region's `AllocationBase` is the module
- Read the four bytes before the address, which hold the module id
> The allocation base of an image region is where the module was loaded, and that is exactly its `HMODULE`.

? What does `VirtualQuery` describe?
+ The run of pages with the same attributes around the address
- Only the single page containing the address
- The whole process
> A region is a run of pages with identical attributes.

? A pointer's region has type `MEM_IMAGE` and protection `PAGE_EXECUTE_READ`. What does it point at?
- A heap block
+ Code inside a loaded exe or DLL
- A thread's stack
> Image regions are modules; execute permission means the code section.

? How do you get from one region to the next when walking the address space?
- Add 4,096 to the address
- Call `VirtualQuery` with a null pointer again
+ Query at `BaseAddress + RegionSize` of the current region
> Regions are consecutive and cover the whole space, free parts included.
```
